    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d829">

                <pb facs="1118" n="1104" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg1104"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/15284: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-04T16:00:00+07:00">March 4, 1944—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 5:06 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>422. Army headquarters here has just informed us of another serious attack on an
                    Arlc [<hi rendition="#italic">Army</hi>] convoy February 19 some 40 miles east
                    of Amichow–Kaiyuan, Yunnan, by 100 to 200 well-armed and apparently
                    well-disciplined men (Embassy despatch 2098, January 31<anchor n="18"
                        xml:id="d829-f1"/> and previous. One American soldier was wounded and
                    certain cargo carried by [convoy], including arms and ammunition, was taken.</p>
                <p>I am seeking appointment with Foreign Minister and will expect to leave with him
                    written representations along lines of last two paragraphs of Department’s
                    telegram 92, January 1 [<hi rendition="#italic">18</hi>], 8 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">10</hi>] p.m.<anchor n="19" xml:id="d829-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>His [<hi rendition="#italic">In</hi>] conversation with Counselor of Embassy on
                    February 26, Political Vice Foreign Minister stated in strictest confidence,
                    referring to attacks of October 19 and January 11, that he himself felt
                    “outraged” over incidents and that Generalissimo was exercised over them but
                    that National Government found itself in a very difficult position <pb
                        facs="1119" n="1105" xml:id="pg1105"/> due to delicacy of its relations with
                    Chairman Lung Yun of Yunnan Provincial Government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
